# Adding a source to this workspace

The engine ships a scraper per big site (AcademicTransfer, EURAXESS, Indeed, LinkedIn) plus
`pages`: a generic, selector-driven scraper you configure per site in `config/sources.yaml`.
Almost every "can you also watch X?" is a new `pages` entry — no engine code, nothing to
install. Never edit the engine inside `.venv`: it is overwritten by the next update.

## 1. Look at the page first

Fetch the vacancy listing page and read its markup. You need one repeating element per
vacancy, and inside it the title and the link. Check what is actually in the HTML the server
returns, not what the rendered page shows — if the vacancies only appear after JavaScript
runs, no selector will find them (go to step 4).

Confirm the site is fine with being read: obey `robots.txt`, and never work around a 403, a
CAPTCHA or a sign-in wall. If the site blocks the fetch, that is a step-4 entry too.

## 2. Write the entry

Under `sources.pages.pages` in `config/sources.yaml`:

```yaml
      - name: Netherlands Institute for Neuroscience   # shown as the source in digests
        url: https://nin.nl/vacancies/
        item: article.vacancies-big-card   # one element per vacancy (required)
        title: h3                          # optional; default: the item's whole text
        link: a.overlay-link               # optional; default: the first <a> in the item
        location: .location                # optional
        deadline: .deadline                # optional; a date is parsed out of the text
        summary: p                         # optional
        allow_empty: true                  # optional; "no open positions" is not an error
```

Prefer a class that describes the vacancy (`article.vacancy`) over layout classes or
`:nth-child`, which break on the next redesign. Relative links are resolved against `url`.

Sites that already post everything on AcademicTransfer (Dutch universities and UMCs) are
usually not worth adding twice.

## 3. Verify it

```bash
uv run jobhunt fetch --source pages
```

Read the output: `pages: N new of M seen`. If M is 0 the `item` selector matched nothing —
go back and look at the markup again. Then check the listings look right:

```bash
uv run jobhunt check   # or just open the Queue in `uv run jobhunt serve`
```

A wrong `title` or `link` selector shows up immediately as junk titles or links that go to
the listing page instead of the vacancy.

## 4. When a selector cannot work

JavaScript-rendered pages, sites that block scrapers, or a page with no list markup at all:

```yaml
      - name: Donders Institute vacancies
        url: https://www.ru.nl/en/working-at/vacancies
        manual: true       # no scraping; the link goes in the digest header to check by hand
```

The user then opens it themselves, and can paste anything worth keeping back in:
`uv run jobhunt add <url>` stores and scores it like any fetched listing.

## 5. A site that needs a real parser

An API, pagination, or a search form that a selector list cannot express belongs in the
engine as a new source module — that is a change to the engine repository, not to this
workspace. Open an issue (the dashboard's *Suggest a feature* link, or
https://github.com/jessefontaine/jobhunt/issues) describing the site and what it lists.

## Reference

| Task | Command |
|------|---------|
| List known sources and whether they are enabled | `uv run jobhunt sources` |
| Fetch one source only | `uv run jobhunt fetch --source <name>` |
| Edit the config in the browser | `uv run jobhunt serve` → Sources |

Enable or disable a whole source with `enabled: true/false` under its name in
`config/sources.yaml`. Queries for the big sites live there too — adding a keyword to
`academictransfer.queries` is often a better first move than adding a new site.
